ric to these by swapping the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).)\n\nWe treat each case.\n\n*Case \\(L:x=2\\).*  \nThe remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1),(2,2)\\setminus\\{(2,1),(2,2)\\}=\n\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nAny pair among these four points is either vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{pair} & \\text{direction}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)-(1,2) & \\text{vertical}\\\\\n(1,1)-(1,3) & \\text{vertical}\\\\\n(1,1)-(3,1) & \\text{horizontal}\\\\\n(1,2)-(1,3) & \\text{vertical}\\\\\n(1,2)-(3,1) & \\text{slope }-1\\\\\n(1,3)-(3,1) & \\text{slope }-1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThus no sunny line can contain two of the remaining points, contradicting the requirement of two sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(L:y=2\\).*  \nThe uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \nTheir pairwise directions are listed in the same table as above (by symmetry), and again every pair is forbidden. Hence impossible.\n\n*Case \\(L:x+y=3\\).*  \nThe uncovered points are \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nCompute slopes:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1)-(1,3):\\text{vertical},\\\\\n&(1,1)-(2,2):\\text{slope }1\\;(\\text{sunny}),\\\\\n&(1,1)-(3,1):\\text{horizontal},\\\\\n&(1,3)-(2,2):\\text{slope }-1,\\\\\n&(1,3)-(3,1):\\text{slope }-1,\\\\\n&(2,2)-(3,1):\\text{slope }-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe only sunny pair is \\((1,1),(2,2)\\), which would be one sunny line.\nThe two points left, \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), are aligned with slope \\(-1\\), a forbidden direction.\nThus we cannot obtain two sunny lines.\n\nAll possibilities for a non‑sunny line containing exactly two points lead to a contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The non‑sunny line contains only one point\n\nIf the unique non‑sunny line passes through a single point of \\(\\mathcal P\\), the other five points must be covered by the two sunny lines.\nEach line can contain at most three points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) (the maximal c