t)\\) and also a point previously covered by \\(y=t\\). Because \\(\\ell_t\\) still contains that diagonal point, no point is lost. Repeating this replacement for exactly \\(k\\) different values of \\(t\\) yields a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) horizontal (non‑sunny) lines. The construction guarantees that the new sunny lines are distinct and that they do not become parallel to the forbidden directions. Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised.\n\n   Therefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nD_{n+1}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(|D_{n+1}|=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Diagonal Lemma)\n\nA sunny line contains at most one point of \\(D_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line contains two distinct points \\((a,b),(a',b')\\in D_{n+1}\\).  \nSince both satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\) and \\(a'+b'=n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b'-b}{a'-a}= \\frac{-(a'-a)}{a'-a}=-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Existence of a covering with any prescribed number of sunny lines)\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover all points of \\(T_n\\);\n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **The case \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   Every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), therefore lies on \\(\\ell_b\\).  \n   None of these lines is sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n2. **The case \\(k=n\\).**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   A_i=(i,n+1-i)\\in D_{n+1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Choose a second point \\(B_i\\) as follows:\n\n   \\[\n   B_i=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   (i+1,1) & 1\\le i\\le n-2,\\\\[2mm]\n   (1,2)   & i=n-1,\\\\[2mm]\n   (1,1)   & i=n .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   Let \\(\\ell_i\\) be the line through \\(A_i\\) and \\(B_i\\).  \n   For \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{slope}(\\ell_i)=\\frac{1-(n+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}=i-n\\in\\{- (n-1),\\dots ,-2\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n\n   For \\(i=n-1\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{slope}(\\ell_{n-1})=\\frac{2-(n+1-(n-1))}{1-(n-1)}\n   =\\frac{2-2}{2-n}=0,\n   \\]\n\n   which is horizontal and therefore **non‑sunny**; to obtain a sunny line we replace \\(B_{n-1}\\) by \\((1,3)\\). The new slope becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3-(n+1-(n-1))}{1-(n-1)}=\\frac{3-2}{2-n}= -\\frac{1}{\\,n-2\\,}\\neq -1,0 .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(i=n\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{slope}(\\ell_{n})=\\frac{1-(n+1-n)}{1-n}= \\frac{0}{1-n}=0,\n   \\]\n\n   again horizontal. Replacing \\(B_n\\) by \\((1,2)\\) gives slope \\(-\\dfrac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq -1,0\\).  \n\n   Thus every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n   Each \\(\\ell_i\\) contains the diagonal point \\(A_i\\); the collection \\(\\{B_i\\}\\) consists of points whose sum is at most \\(n\\). Any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) either belongs to \\(D_{n+1}\\) (hence lies on the corresponding \\(\\ell_i\\)) or belongs to some \\(B_j\\) (hence lies on \\(\\ell_j\\)). Consequently the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all of \\(T_n\\). Hence \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n3. **Intermediate values \\(0<k<n\\).**  \n   Begin with the all‑horizontal covering of Lemma 2‑1 (\\(k=0\\)).  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) replace the horizontal line \\(y=t\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_t\\) constructed in the proof of the case \\(k=n\\) (the line that contains the diagonal point \\((t,n+1-t)\\)).  \n\n   Because \\(\\ell_t\\) still passes through \\((t,n+1-t)\\), every point that was previously covered by the horizontal line \\(y=t\\) remains covered: the points with \\(y=t\\) are exactly the points of the form \\((a,t)\\) with \\(a\\le n+1-t\\); each such point also lies on the line \\(\\ell_a\\) (the sunny line associated to the diagonal point \\((a,n+1-a)\\)).  \n\n   Performing this replacement for exactly \\(k\\) distinct values of \\(t\\) yields a configuration consisting of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines, still covering all points of \\(T_n\\). The lines are distinct because the horizontal lines have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, while the sunny lines have distinct slopes (see the explicit formulas above).  \n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemma 1 shows that a sunny line can meet the “top diagonal’’ \\(D_{n+1}\\) in at most one point, but Lemma 2 demonstrates that this restriction does **not** limit the number of sunny lines: we can realise any number from \\(0\\) up to \\(n\\). Therefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
