and also a point previously covered by \\(y=t\\). Because \\(\\ell_t\\) still contains that diagonal point, no point is lost. Repeating this replacement for exactly \\(k\\) different values of \\(t\\) yields a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) horizontal (non‑sunny) lines. The construction guarantees that the new sunny lines are distinct and that they do not become parallel to the forbidden directions. Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised.\n\n   Therefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nD_{n+1}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(|D_{n+1}|=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Diagonal Lemma)\n\nA sunny line contains at most one point of \\(D_{n+1}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line contains two distinct points \\((a,b),(a',b')\\in D_{n+1}\\).  \nSince both satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\) and \\(a'+b'=n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b'-b}{a'-a}= \\frac{-(a'-a)}{a'-a}=-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Existence of a covering with any prescribed number of sunny lines)\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover all points of \\(T_n\\);\n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **The case \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   Every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), therefore lies on \\(\\ell_b\\).  \n   None of these lines is sunny, so \\(k=0\\).\n\n2. **The case \\(k=n\\)