\(\\ell_t\\) still passes through \\((t,n+1-t)\\), every point that was previously covered by the horizontal line \\(y=t\\) remains covered: the points with \\(y=t\\) are exactly the points of the form \\((a,t)\\) with \\(a\\le n+1-t\\); each such point also lies on the line \\(\\ell_a\\) (the sunny line associated to the diagonal point \\((a,n+1-a)\\)).  \n\n   Performing this replacement for exactly \\(k\\) distinct values of \\(t\\) yields a configuration consisting of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines, still covering all points of \\(T_n\\). The lines are distinct because the horizontal lines have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, while the sunny lines have distinct slopes (see the explicit formulas above).  \n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemma 1 shows that a sunny line can meet the “top diagonal’’ \\(D_{n+1}\\) in at most one point, but Lemma 2 demonstrates that this restriction does **not** limit the number of sunny lines: we can realise any number from \\(0\\) up to \\(n\\). Therefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
