            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nD_n=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\n      =\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Diagonal Lemma)\n\nA sunny line contains at most one point of \\(D_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line contains two distinct points \\((a,b),(a',b')\\in D_n\\).  \nSince both satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\) and \\(a'+b'=n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b'-b}{a'-a}\n   =\\frac{-(a'-a)}{a'-a}=-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Covering with all sunny lines (\\(k=n\\))\n\n#### Construction\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define a line \\(\\ell_i\\) by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{the line through }(i,n+1-i)\\text{ and }(i+1,1), & 1\\le i\\le n-2,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{the line through }(n-1,2)\\text{ and }(1,3),      & i=n-1,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{the line through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(1,2),      & i=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) we replace the three lines in (1) by the explicit lines\n\\((1,3)-(2,1),\\;(2,2)-(1,1),\\;(3,1)-(1,2)\\); they are the same as (1) after\nrenaming the indices.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \n\nAll lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) the slope equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-(n+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}=i-n\\le-2,\n\\]\n\nhence it is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n\nFor \\(i=n-1\\) the slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{3-2}{1-(n-1)}=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-2\\,},\n\\]\n\nwhich is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for every \\(n\\ge4\\); the case \\(n=3\\) is handled\nseparately.  \n\nFor \\(i=n\\) the slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2-1}{1-n}=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,},\n\\]\n\nagain different from the three forbidden values. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \n\n\\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nB_1=\\{(i+1,1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{(n,1)\\},\n\\qquad\nB_2=\\{(1,2),(1,3)\\},\n\\qquad\nI=T_n\\setminus\\bigl(D_n\\cup B_1\\cup B_2\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n*Points of \\(B_1\\).*  \nIf \\((i+1,1)\\in B_1\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) then, by definition of \\(\\ell_i\\),\n\\((i+1,1)\\in\\ell_i\\).  The point \\((n,1)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_n\\).\n\n*Points of \\(B_2\\).*  \n\\((1,2)\\in\\ell_n\\) and \\((1,3)\\in\\ell_{n-1}\\) by the definition of the last two\nlines in (1).\n\n*Interior points.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in I\\).  Then \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently\n\\(a\\le n-1\\).  Put \\(i=a\\).  Because \\(i\\le n-2\\) we are in the first case of\n(1); the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\ny-1=(i-n)\\bigl(x-(i+1)\\bigr).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a=i\\) and \\(y=b\\) into (2) gives  \n\n\\[\nb-1=(i-n)(i-(i+1))=(i-n)(-1)=n-i-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is equivalent to \\(a+b=n+1\\).  Since \\((a,b)\\in I\\) we have\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\); the equality holds precisely because of the computation\nabove, hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (2) and therefore lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\ell_i\\).\nThe slopes of the \\(\\ell_i\\) are pairwise different (they are\n\\(\\{i-n\\mid1\\le i\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{-\\frac1{n-2},-\\frac1{n-1}\\}\\)), so the lines\nare distinct. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Covering with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny and clearly every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies\n\\(1\\le b\\le n\\); hence it lies on the line \\(y=b\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  From \\(k=n\\) to any intermediate \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) be the all‑sunny covering of Lemma 3.\nFix a subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|S|=n-k\\) and replace, for each\n\\(i\\in S\\), the line \\(\\ell_i\\) by the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nh_i:\\;y=n+1-i .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((i,n+1-i)\\in D_n\\) lies on both \\(\\ell_i\\) and \\(h_i\\); therefore the\nreplacement does not lose that diagonal point.\n\nIt remains to check that no other point of \\(T_n\\) is lost.\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) that is not on a line \\(h_i\\) has a \\(y\\)-coordinate\ndifferent from the numbers \\(n+1-i\\;(i\\in S)\\).  Such a point was covered by a\nline \\(\\ell_j\\) with \\(j\\notin S\\) (otherwise it would have been on a line\n\\(\\ell_i\\) with \\(i\\in S\\) and consequently its \\(y\\)-coordinate would be\n\\(n+1-i\\)).  Since the lines \\(\\ell_j\\) with \\(j\\notin S\\) are left unchanged,\nthe point remains covered.\n\nAll the new horizontal lines are distinct because the numbers\n\\(n+1-i\\;(i\\in S)\\) are distinct, and they are distinct from the unchanged\nsunny lines because a sunny line never has slope \\(0\\).  Consequently the final\nfamily consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the unchanged \\(\\ell_i\\)) and\n\\(n-k\\) non