            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nD_n=\\{(i,n+1-i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Diagonal Lemma)\n\nA sunny line contains at most one point of \\(D_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a line contains two distinct points \\((i,n+1-i)\\) and \\((j,n+1-j)\\) of \\(D_n\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \nIts slope equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n+1-j)-(n+1-i)}{j-i}\n   =\\frac{-(j-i)}{j-i}=-1,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting the definition of a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  Thus the \\(n\\) lines cover \\(T_n\\) and contain exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define a line \\(\\ell_i\\) by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{the line through }(i,n+1-i)\\text{ and }(i+1,1), & 1\\le i\\le n-2,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{the line through }(n-1,2)\\text{ and }(1,3),      & i=n-1,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{the line through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(1,2),      & i=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nAll lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) the slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-(n+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}=i-n\\le-2,\n\\]\n\nhence different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nFor \\(i=n-1\\) the slope is \\(-\\dfrac{1}{\\,n-2\\,}\\); for \\(i=n\\) it is\n\\(-\\dfrac{1}{\\,n-1\\,}\\).  Neither equals \\(0,\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \nThe family \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDecompose \\(T_n\\) as  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nB_1&=\\{(i+1,1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-2\\}\\cup\\{(n,1)\\},\\\\\nB_2&=\\{(1,2),(1,3)\\},\\\\\nI   &=T_n\\setminus\\bigl(D_n\\cup B_1\\cup B_2\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Points of \\(B_1\\).*  By definition, \\((i+1,1)\\in\\ell_i\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\) and \\((n,1)\\in\\ell_n\\).\n\n*Points of \\(B_2\\).*  \\((1,2)\\in\\ell_n\\) and \\((1,3)\\in\\ell_{n-1}\\).\n\n*Points of the diagonal \\(D_n\\).*  By construction each \\((i,n+1-i)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n*Interior points.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in I\\).  Then \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\); consequently \\(a\\le n-1\\).  Set \\(i=a\\).  Because\n\\(i\\le n-2\\) we are in the first case of (4); the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\ny-1=(i-n)\\bigl(x-(i+1)\\bigr). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a=i\\) and \\(y=b\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb-1=(i-n)(i-(i+1))=(i-n)(-1)=n-i-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is equivalent to \\(a+b=n+1\\).  Since \\((a,b)\\in I\\) we have\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\); the equality obtained above shows that (5) holds,\nhence \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\).  The slopes listed in Lemma 2 are pairwise\ndifferent, so the lines are distinct. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The case \\(0<k<n\\)\n\nStart from the all‑sunny covering \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) of Lemma 3.\nChoose any subset \\(S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|S|=n-k\\).\nFor each \\(i\\in S\\) replace the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) by the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nh_i:\\;y=n+1-i . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((i,n+1-i)\\in D_n\\) lies on both \\(\\ell_i\\) and \\(h_i\\); therefore the\nreplacement does not lose any diagonal point.  \n\nConsider a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) that was covered by a line \\(\\ell_i\\) with\n\\(i\\in S\\) but does **not** belong to the new horizontal line \\(h_i\\).  Then\n\\(b\\neq n+1-i\\).  Because \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\), Lemma 1 tells us that\n\\((a,b)\\) cannot be another point of \\(D_n\\); hence \\(a\\neq i\\).  Consequently\n\\((a,b)\\) also lies on the line \\(\\ell_a\\) (the line indexed by its\n\\(x\\)-coordinate) and, since \\(a\\notin S\\) (otherwise \\(\\ell_a\\) would have\nbeen replaced), \\(\\ell_a\\) is still present in the family.  Therefore the\npoint remains covered.\n\nThe new lines (6) are distinct because they have different \\(y\\)-coordinates,\nand each of them is non‑sunny (horizontal).  The unchanged lines \\(\\ell_j\\)\nwith \\(j\\notin S\\) remain sunny.  Hence the final collection consists of\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines and still covers all of\n\\(T_n\\).\n\nSince the subset \\(S\\) can be chosen arbitrarily, a configuration exists for\nevery integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited explicit constructions for\n\n* \\(k=0\\) (all lines horizontal);\n* \\(k=n\\) (the family \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) of Lemma 3);\n* every intermediate \\(k\\) (by replacing \\(n-k\\) of the s