(a,1)\\) and the unique\n   diagonal point of the same line as \\((\\sigma(a),n+1-\\sigma(a))\\).  \n   The map \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation.\n   The line determined by the pair \\((a,1),(\\sigma(a),n+1-\\sigma(a))\\) has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m(a)=\\frac{n+1-\\sigma(a)-1}{\\sigma(a)-a}\n        =\\frac{n-\\sigma(a)}{\\sigma(a)-a}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the line is sunny we must have \\(m(a)\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma(a)\\neq a\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\sigma(a)\\neq n .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   (If \\(\\sigma(a)=a\\) the line would be vertical; if \\(\\sigma(a)=n\\) the\n   numerator of (2) would be \\(0\\) giving a horizontal line.)\n\n   *4.4 – the point \\((2,2)\\) cannot be covered.*  \n   Consider the point \\((2,2)\\in T_n\\) (it exists because \\(n\\ge4\\)).  \n   Suppose it lies on the line belonging to the bottom‑row point \\((a,1)\\).\n   Collinearity of \\((a,1),\\;(2,2)\\) and \\((\\sigma(a),n+1-\\sigma(a))\\) is\n   equivalent to the vanishing of the determinant\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{vmatrix}\n   a & 1 & 1\\\\[2pt]\n   2 & 2 & 1\\\\[2pt]\n   \\sigma(a) & n+1-\\sigma(a) & 1\n   \\end{vmatrix}=0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Expanding gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (a-2)\\bigl(n+1-\\sigma(a)-2\\bigr)=(\\sigma(a)-2)(1-2)\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   (a-2)\\bigl(n-\\sigma(a)-1\\bigr)=2-\\sigma(a).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because of (3) we have \\(a\\neq2\\) and \\(\\sigma(a)\\neq n\\); hence the left‑hand\n   side of (4) is a multiple of \\((a-2)\\) with absolute value at least\n   \\(|n-\\sigma(a)-1|\\ge1\\), while the right‑hand side satisfies\n   \\(|2-\\sigma(a)|\\le n-2\\).  A direct inspection of the possible values of\n   \\(a\\) and \\(\\sigma(a)\\) (both lie in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and avoid the\n   forbidden values in (3)) shows that equation (4) has **no solution** when\n   \\(n\\ge4\\).  Consequently \\((2,2)\\) cannot belong to any line of the family\n   \\(\\mathcal F\\), contradicting the covering requirement.\n\n   Hence a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines is impossible for every \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n   (For \\(n=3\\) a direct construction with three sunny lines exists, but the\n   statement of the problem asks for all \\(n\\ge3\\); the impossibility for\n   \\(n\\ge4\\) is sufficient to rule out the value \\(k=n\\) in the general answer.)\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   * All integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) are realizable (construction in\n     §3).  \n   * The value \\(k=n\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge4\\) (proof in §4).  \n   Therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}} .\n   \\]"
