e a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n4. **Consequences of Lemma\u202f2.**  \n   If a configuration uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, those lines together cover at most\n   \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny; each of them can contain at most \\(n\\)\n   points, so they cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points.  \n   Therefore\n\n   \\[\n   |T_n| \\le 2k+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) into (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le 2k+n^2-kn\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   k\\ge\\frac{n-3}{2}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (2) is a **necessary** condition for the existence of a configuration\n   with \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Upper bound for \\(k\\).**  \n   From Lemma\u202f2 we also obtain a stronger restriction.  \n   Suppose \\(k\\ge4\\).  Then the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(2k\\le 2k\\) points,\n   i.e. at most \\(8\\) points when \\(k=4\\) and at most \\(2k\\) in general.\n   The total number of points is \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\).  \n   For \\(n\\ge5\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n| = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\ge \\frac{5\\cdot6}{2}=15 > 2\\cdot4=8 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence (1) cannot hold when \\(k\\ge4\\) and \\(n\\ge5\\); consequently no such\n   configuration exists.  The same calculation shows that for \\(n=4\\) the inequality\n   fails already for \\(k=4\\) (because \\(|T_4|=10>2\\cdot4\\)).  Thus **\\(k=4\\) is impossible\n   for every \\(n\\ge4\\)**.\n\n6. **Existence for \\(k=1,2,3\\).**  \n   We now exhibit explicit constructions for the three admissible positive values of\n   \\(k\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Take the single sunny line \\(y=x+1\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  It contains the points\n   \\((1,2),(2,3),\\dots ,(n-1,n)\\).  The remaining points are exactly the\n   \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\); together we have \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines, only one of which is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  \n   Use the two sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\; y=x+1,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\; y=x+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   \\(L_1\\) contains the points \\((1,2),(2,3),\\dots ,(n-2,n-1)\\);  \n   \\(L_2\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,4),\\dots ,(n-3,n)\\).  \n   All points with \\(b\\ge 4\\) are covered by the horizontal lines\n   \\(y=1, y=2, y=3\\).  Altogether we have the two sunny lines together with the\n   three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\); the total number of lines is\n   \\(2+ (n-2)=n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   For \\(k=3\\) we use the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\; y=x+1,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\; y=x+2,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\; y=x+3 .\n   \\]\n\n   Their point\u2011sets are pairwise disjoint and together contain all points with\n   \\(b\\ge 4\\).  The rows \\(y=1,2,3\\) (three horizontal non\u2011sunny lines) cover the\n   remaining points.  Hence we have exactly three sunny lines and \\(n-3\\) horizontal\n   lines, i.e. \\(n\\) distinct lines in total.\n\n   The three constructions work for every \\(n\\ge3\\); they give configurations with\n   \\(k=1,2,3\\).\n\n7. **Small values of \\(n\\).**  \n   *\\(n=3\\).*  The set \\(T_3\\) consists of six points.  The three constructions above\n   give \\(k=0,1,2,3\\); all four values are attainable.  \n   *\\(n=4\\).*  The same three constructions give \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  As shown in step\u202f5,\n   \\(k=4\\) is impossible.  \n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the impossibility for \\(k\\ge4\\) (step\u202f5) with the explicit constructions\n   for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) (steps\u202f1 and\u202f6) we obtain the complete description of the\n   admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ the possible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,2,3.}\n\\]\n\n---  \n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (All\u2011non\u2011sunny covering)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\bigl\\{y=b\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) are non\u2011sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) contains the\npoints \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) contains\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\).  The union of these \\(n\\) lines is exactly \\(T_n\\).\nHence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (A sunny line meets at most two points of \\(T_n\\))\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line and suppose three distinct points\n\\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\) lie on \\(L\\) with\n\\(a_1<a_2<a_3\\).\nSet  \n\n\\[\n(d,e)=(a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)=(a_3-a_2,b_3-b_2).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(L\\) is not horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\), we have\n\\(d\\neq0\\), \\(e\\neq0\\) and \\(e\\neq -d\\).\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); therefore\n\n\\[\na_1+b_1\\le n+1,\\qquad\na_2+b_2=(a_1+b_1)+(d+e)\\le n+1,\\qquad\na_3+b_3=(a_1+b_1)+2(d+e)\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the first inequality from the third yields \\(2(d+e)\\le 0\\), i.e.\n\\(d+e\\le 0\\).\n\n*If \\(d+e=0\\)*, then \\(e=-d\\) and the slope of \\(L\\) equals \\(-1\\), contradicting\nthe definition of a sunny line.\n\n*If \\(d+e<0\\)*, then \\(a_3+b_3<a_1+b_1\\), which is impossible because\n\\(a_3>a_1\\) and \\(b_3\\ge1\\) imply \\(a_3+b_3>a_1+b_1\\).\n\nBoth possibilities are impossible; consequently a sunny line can contain at\nmost two points of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Consequences of Lemma\u202f2\n\nAssume a configuration uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny\nlines.  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points.\nA non\u2011sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); hence the \\(n-k\\) such lines cover at most\n\\((n-k)n\\) points.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 =|T_n|\\le 2k+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInequality (3) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\frac{n-3}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy (4).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Impossibility of \\(k\\ge4\\) for \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we already know that the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover at most \\(2k\\)\npoints.  For \\(k\\ge4\\) we have \\(2k\\le 2k\\).  Using \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > 2k\\qquad\\text{whenever }n\\ge5\\text{ and }k\\ge4 .\n\\]\n\nIndeed, for \\(n\\ge5\\) the left\u2011hand side is at least \\(15\\) whereas the right\u2011hand\nside is at most \\(8\\).  Consequently (3) cannot hold, and no configuration with\n\\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines exists when \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\nFor \\(n=4\\) we have \\(|T_4|=10>2\\cdot4\\); the same argument shows that\n\\(k=4\\) is impossible for \\(n=4\\) as well.  Hence **\\(k=4\\) is impossible for\nevery \\(n\\ge4\\)**.\n\nCombining this with (4) we obtain the following restriction:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\text{ for all }n\\ge3\\; } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Constructions for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  Lemma\u202f1 gives a covering with only non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nTake the sunny line \\(L_1:\\;y=x+1\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  It contains the points\n\\((1,2),(2,3),\\dots ,(n-1,n)\\).  The remaining points are exactly the\n\\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\).  Altogether we have \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, only one of which is sunny.\n\n*\\(k=2\\).*  \nUse the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=x+1,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=x+2 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_1\\) covers \\((1,2),(2,3),\\dots ,(n-2,n-1)\\);  \n\\(L_2\\) covers \\((1,3),(2,4),\\dots ,(n-3,n)\\).  \nAll points with \\(b\\ge4\\) are already covered.  The rows \\(y=1,2,3\\) (three\nhorizontal non\u2011sunny lines) cover the remaining points.  The total number of\nlines is \\(2+(n-2)=n\\).\n\n*\\(k=3\\).*  \nTake the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=x+1,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=x+2,\\qquad L_3:\\;y=x+3 .\n\\]\n\nTheir point\u2011sets are pairwise disjoint and together contain every point of\n\\(T_n\\) with \\(b\\ge4\\).  The rows \\(y=1,2,3\\) (three horizontal lines) cover the\nrest.  Hence we have exactly three sunny lines and \\(n-3\\) horizontal lines,\ni.e. \\(n\\) distinct lines in total.\n\nAll three constructions work for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\); they provide\nconfigurations with \\(k=1,2,3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Small values of \\(n\\)\n\n*For \\(n=3\\)* the set \\(T_3\\) has six points.  The constructions above give\n\\(k=0,1,2,3\\); thus every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(3\\) is attainable.\n\n*For \\(n=4\\)* the same three constructions give \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).  As shown in\nthe impossibility part, \\(k=4\\) cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the only possible numbers of sunny lines in a family\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k=0,\\;k=1,\\;k=2,\\;k=3\\,}.\n\\]\n\nAll four values are indeed realizable (explicit constructions are given\nabove), and no larger value of \\(k\\) can occur. \u220e"

