"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I was not able to determine the complete set of admissible values of\u202f\\(k\\).  \nNevertheless I have obtained rigorous partial results:\n\n*   For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration with **\\(k=0\\)** sunny lines.  \n*   For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration with **\\(k=1\\)** sunny line.  \n*   For any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThe first two items give the exact attainable values \\(k=0,1\\); the third item is a non\u2011trivial upper bound that rules out the possibility \\(k=n-1\\) (and therefore \\(k=n\\)).  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f1 (All\u2011non\u2011sunny covering).**  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_0=\\bigl\\{y=b\\mid b=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{x+y=n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Hence \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f2 (A construction with one sunny line).**  \n   For any \\(n\\ge3\\) the following \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(T_n\\):\n\n   *   Sunny line\u2003\\(L:\\;y=x+1\\) (slope \\(1\\));\n   *   Horizontal lines\u2003\\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=n-2\\) (that is, \\(n-2\\) lines);\n   *   Diagonal line\u2003\\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\n   The total number of lines is \\(1+(n-2)+1=n\\).  \n   *Coverage check.*  \n   \u2013 If a point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b-a=1\\) it lies on \\(L\\).  \n   \u2013 If \\(b\\le n-2\\) it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n   \u2013 If \\(b\\ge n-1\\) then \\(a\\le 2\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \n     When \\(a=1\\) we have \\(a+b=n+1\\) and the point is on the diagonal;  \n     when \\(a=2\\) we have \\(b=n-1\\) and again \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the listed lines, and exactly one of the lines (the first) is sunny. Consequently \\(k=1\\) is always attainable.\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f3 (Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\)).**  \n   Consider the first column of the triangle,\n   \\[\n   C_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}.\n   \\]\n   A sunny line is not vertical, therefore it meets \\(C_1\\) in **at most one** point.  \n   Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines together can cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(C_1\\).  \n   The remaining \\(n-k\\) points of \\(C_1\\) must be covered by the non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   The only non\u2011sunny line that can contain more than one point of \\(C_1\\) is the vertical line \\(x=1\\); every other non\u2011sunny line (horizontal or diagonal) meets \\(C_1\\) in at most one point.  \n\n   Consequently, if the vertical line \\(x=1\\) is **not** used, we would need at least \\(n-k\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines just to cover the uncovered points of \\(C_1\\).  Since we have exactly \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, the only way to avoid a contradiction is to use the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n\n   The vertical line \\(x=1\\) consumes one of the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, leaving at most \\(n-k-1\\) non\u2011sunny lines to cover the remaining points of the triangle.  All those remaining points lie in the sub\u2011triangle obtained by deleting the first column; this sub\u2011triangle contains \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\) points.  Each of the remaining non\u2011sunny lines can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points (the maximal length of a horizontal, vertical or diagonal line inside the sub\u2011triangle).  Therefore\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\;\\le\\;(n-k-1)(n-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Dividing by \\(n-1>0\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n}{2}\\;\\le\\;n-k-1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **no configuration can have \\(k\\ge n-1\\)** sunny lines.\n\nThese three lemmas constitute the rigorous partial solution described above. \u220e"

