            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-1\\, } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of those lines are sunny; no configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists when \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound.**  \n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   *Proof.* Write the line as \\(y=\\frac{u}{v}x+q\\) with coprime integers \\(u,v\\) (\\(v>0\\)) and \\(\\frac{u}{v}\\neq0,-1\\).  \n   Integer points on the line satisfy \\(u x\\equiv -v q\\pmod v\\); hence the admissible \\(x\\)-coordinates form an arithmetic progression with step \\(v\\).  \n   Because \\(1\\le x\\le n\\), at most \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{v}\\bigr\\rceil\\) values occur.  \n   Since \\(\\frac{u}{v}\\neq -1\\), we have \\(|u|\\neq v\\); consequently \\(v\\ge 2\\) and \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{v}\\bigr\\rceil\\le\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\). ∎  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines. The remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny, each of which can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1\\qquad\\text{for }n\\ge4,\n   \\]\n   while for \\(n=3\\) inequality (1) allows \\(k\\le 3\\).  \n   If \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny) then the left‑hand side of (1) is at most \\(n\\lceil n/2\\rceil <\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) for every \\(n\\ge4\\); hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) is impossible when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n3. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_0=\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\}\\cup\\{x+y=n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   are all non‑sunny and cover \\(T_n\\) (the vertical line \\(x=c\\) covers the whole column \\(c\\); the diagonal covers the unique point \\((n,1)\\)). Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n4. **Inductive construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Assume that for some \\(m\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m-2\\) there exists a suitable family of \\(m\\) lines.  \n   For \\(n=m+1\\) and a prescribed \\(k\\le n-2\\) we take the diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1\n   \\]\n\n   (non‑sunny) and apply the inductive hypothesis to the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{n-1}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is exactly the set of points of \\(T_n\\) not lying on \\(D\\).  \n   The induction supplies \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n-1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Adding \\(D\\) yields \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all of \\(T_n\\) and still with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   The base case \\(n=3\\) is verified directly (see §5).  \n   Hence for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration exists.\n\n5. **Explicit construction for the maximal value \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1\n   \\]\n\n   (non‑sunny).  The remaining points form the triangle \\(T_{n-1}\\) of size \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\).  \n   For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(1\\le s\\le n-1\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_s:\\; y = x + s .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the slope is \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(L_s\\) is sunny.  \n   The integer points of \\(T_{n-1}\\) satisfying \\(y-x=s\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl\\{(x,\\,x+s)\\mid 1\\le x\\le \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-s}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(|L_s\\cap T_{n-1}|=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-s}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   Summing over \\(s=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{s=1}^{n-1}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-s}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\frac{(n-1)n}{2},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the sets \\(L_s\\cap T_{n-1}\\) partition \\(T_{n-1}\\).  \n   Consequently the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_s\\}_{s=1}^{n-1}\\) together cover every point of \\(T_{n-1}\\) and are pairwise distinct.  \n   Adding the diagonal \\(D\\) gives a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all of \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Verification for the smallest case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The three admissible values are realised by the following families:\n\n   * \\(k=0\\): \\(\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x+y=4\\}\\);\n   * \\(k=1\\): \\(\\{y=1,\\;x=1,\\;y=x\\}\\);\n   * \\(k=2\\): \\(\\{y=x,\\;y=x+1,\\;x=1\\}\\).\n\n   All three families satisfy the covering condition.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The upper bound of Lemma 1 together with the explicit constructions of §§3–5 shows that for every \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\n   \\[\n   \\{k\\mid\\text{a configuration exists}\\}= \\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   No configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists when \\(n\\ge4\\); for \\(n=3\\) the bound also excludes \\(k=3\\) because a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxe