\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n\\ge |T_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Because \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le n-1\\); solving (1) gives\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Thus a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny is impossible for every\n   \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Existence for \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).**  \n   We prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n   *Base \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The three families displayed in \u00a7\u202f6 of the detailed solution verify the\n   statement for \\(k=0,1,2\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement true for some \\(n-1\\ge3\\).  Take a configuration for\n   \\(n-1\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines; it covers the set\n   \\(T_{n-1}\\).  Add the diagonal line\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1 ,\n   \\]\n   which is non\u2011sunny and contains precisely the points of \\(T_n\\setminus\n   T_{n-1}\\).  The resulting family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n   still has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covers all of \\(T_n\\).  Hence the\n   statement holds for \\(n\\).\n\n   Consequently for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\le n-2\\) a required family\n   exists.\n\n5. **Construction for the maximal value \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   We exhibit a family with exactly one non\u2011sunny line and \\(n-1\\) sunny\n   lines.\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny line.*  Take the vertical line\n   \\[\n   V:\\;x=1 .\n   \\]\n   It covers the \\(n\\) points \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) define\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; \\text{the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(i+1,n-i) .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   (When \\(i=n\\) we interpret \\((n+1,n-n)= (n+1,0)\\) and replace (2) by the\n   line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); this replacement is described below.)\n\n   *Verification that the lines are sunny.*  \n   For \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) the two defining points satisfy\n   \\[\n   (i+1)+(n-i)=n+1 ,\n   \\]\n   so the second point lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  The slope of \\(L_i\\) is\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{n-i-1}{\\,i+1-i\\,}=n-i-1 .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(i\\le n-1\\) we have \\(m_i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) is\n   sunny.  For the last column we use\n   \\[\n   L_n:\\; \\text{the line through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(1,2),\n   \\]\n   whose slope is \\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-2\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(L_n\\) is also\n   sunny.\n\n   *Distinctness.*  The slopes \\(\\{m_i\\}_{i=2}^{n-1}\\) are the distinct\n   integers \\(n-3,n-4,\\dots ,0\\); together with the slope of \\(L_n\\) we obtain\n   \\(n-1\\) different slopes, so the lines are pairwise distinct.\n\n   *Coverage of all points.*  \n   \u2013 Every point with \\(x=1\\) lies on \\(V\\).  \n   \u2013 For a point \\((i,1)\\) with \\(i\\ge2\\) the line \\(L_i\\) contains it by\n   definition.  \n   \u2013 For a point \\((i,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\) we have \\(i+b\\le n+1\\); therefore\n   the point \\((i,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) together with the\n   point \\((i+1,n-i)\\).  Both points are on the same line \\(L_i\\); consequently\n   \\((i,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_i\\).  (Indeed, the three points \\((i,1)\\),\n   \\((i,b)\\) and \\((i+1,n-i)\\) are collinear because they satisfy the linear\n   relation \\(y-1=(n-i-1)(x-i)\\).)\n\n   Hence every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to \\(V\\) or to one of the\n   lines \\(L_i\\;(2\\le i\\le n)\\).  The family consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines, of which precisely \\(n-1\\) are sunny.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 together with inequality (2) shows that \\(k\\le n-1\\) is necessary.\n   The inductive construction supplies configurations for all\n   \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\); the explicit family of \u00a7\u202f5 provides a configuration\n   for \\(k=n-1\\).  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\n   \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }\nk=0,1,\\dots ,n-1.}\n\\]"

