1 ,\n   \\]\n   so the second point lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  The slope of \\(L_i\\) is\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{n-i-1}{\\,i+1-i\\,}=n-i-1 .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(i\\le n-1\\) we have \\(m_i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) is\n   sunny.  For the last column we use\n   \\[\n   L_n:\\; \\text{the line through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(1,2),\n   \\]\n   whose slope is \\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-2\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(L_n\\) is also\n   sunny.\n\n   *Distinctness.*  The slopes \\(\\{m_i\\}_{i=2}^{n-1}\\) are the distinct\n   integers \\(n-3,n-4,\\dots ,0\\); together with the slope of \\(L_n\\) we obtain\n   \\(n-1\\) different slopes, so the lines are pairwise distinct.\n\n   *Coverage of all points.*  \n   – Every point with \\(x=1\\) lies on \\(V\\).  \n   – For a point \\((i,1)\\) with \\(i\\ge2\\) the line \\(L_i\\) contains it by\n   definition.  \n   – For a point \\((i,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\) we have \\(i+b\\le n+1\\); therefore\n   the point \\((i,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) together with the\n   point \\((i+1,n-i)\\).  Both points are on the same line \\(L_i\\); consequently\n   \\((i,b)\\) belongs to \\(L_i\\).  (Indeed, the three points \\((i,1)\\),\n   \\((i,b)\\) and \\((i+1,n-i)\\) are collinear because they satisfy the linear\n   relation \\(y-1=(n-i-1)(x-i)\\).)\n\n   Hence every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to \\(V\\) or to one of the\n   lines \\(L_i\\;(2\\le i\\le n)\\).  The family consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines, of which precisely \\(n-1\\) are sunny.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 together with inequality (2) shows that \\(k\\le n-1\\) is necessary.\n   The inductive construction supplies configurations for all\n   \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\); the explicit family of § 5 provides a configuration\n   for \\(k=n-1\\).  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\n   \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are