olution ###\n\n#### 1.  Notation and basic facts  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) define  \n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nAll lines considered are distinct; two lines are distinct iff they have different slopes or, when the slopes coincide, different intercepts.\n\n#### 2.  Lemma 1 – a bound for a sunny line  \n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n\\[\n|L\\cap S_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the line in reduced rational form\n\\[\nL:\\;y=\\frac uv\\,x+q ,\\qquad u,v\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;v>0,\\;\\gcd(u,v)=1,\n\\]\nwith \\(\\frac uv\\neq0,-1\\).  \n\nFor an integer point \\((x,y)\\in L\\) we have\n\\[\ny=\\frac uv x+q\\in\\mathbb Z\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;ux\\equiv -vq\\pmod v .\n\\]\nHence the admissible \\(x\\)-coordinates form an arithmetic progression with common difference \\(v\\).  \nSince \\(1\\le x\\le n\\), at most \\(\\lceil n/v\\rceil\\) values of \\(x\\) occur.  \n\nIf \\(v\\ge2\\) then \\(\\lceil n/v\\rceil\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) and we are done.\n\nAssume \\(v=1\\). Then the slope is an integer \\(m=u\\neq0,-1\\) and the line has equation \\(y=mx+q\\) with \\(q\\in\\mathbb Z\\).  \nFor a point of \\(S_n\\) we need\n\\[\n1\\le x\\le n,\\qquad 1\\le mx+q\\le n+1-x .\n\\]\nFrom the two inequalities we obtain\n\\[\n(m+1)x\\le n+1-q\\quad\\text{and}\\quad mx+q\\ge1 .\n\\]\nBecause \\(m\\neq0,-1\\) we have \\(|m+1|\\ge2\\); consequently\n\\[\nx\\le\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\nThus at most \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) integer values of \\(x\\) satisfy the conditions, and the lemma follows. ∎\n\n\n\n#### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a configuration consist of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny line